what's the difference between a camel and dromedary,"['https://www.christmastraditions.com/product/WLD-001.html', 'http://rockwellantiquesdallas.com/syrian-brass-inlaid-camel-saddle/', 'https://m.dailyjigsawpuzzles.net/animal-jigsaws/green-chameleon_1039.html', 'https://coloringpagebase.com/collection/camel-coloring-page/', 'https://www.canyousendmeapostcard.com/tunisia-98/a-postcard-from-tunisia-emilia-1172.html', 'http://www.pedegru.com/Grupedia/camel', 'http://wildlife-animals.com/camel/camel-pacifiers.php']","A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus, bearing distinctive fatty deposits known as ""humps"" on its back. The three surviving species of camel are the dromedary, or one-humped camel (C. dromedarius), which inhabits the Middle East and the Horn of Africa; the Bactrian, or two-humped camel (C. bactrianus), which inhabits Central Asia; and the critically endangered wild Bactrian camel (C. ferus) that has limited populations in remote areas of northwest China and Mongolia. Bactrian camels take their name from the historical Bactria region of Central Asia. Additionally one other species of camel in the separate genus Camelops, C. hesternus lived in western North America and became extinct when humans entered the continent at the end of the Pleistocene. Both the dromedary and the Bactrian camels have been domesticated; they provide milk, meat, hair for textiles or goods such as felted pouches, and are working animals with tasks ranging from human transport to bearing loads.","404 Not Found – Traditions 404 Page Not Found The page you were looking for could not be found. It might have been removed, renamed, or did not exist in the first place. Perhaps searching can help. What are You looking for ... Search Store Reviews by Real Customers: Sign up for our newsletter! E-mail Subscribe Choosing a selection results in a full page refresh. Free Shipping Free Standard Shipping with your online purchase of $99 or more (merchandise subtotal is calculated b