who holds the title in a land contract,"['http://metaglossary.com/define/assumption', 'https://www.homesweethomeinvestments.com/notes.html', 'http://pelangsingbadan.info/news.php?action=show&id=18018', 'http://www.turnkeyproperties.us/articles-and-information/previous/2', 'http://www.trustanswers.com/land-contract-explain', 'http://cashnoteusa.com/blog/page/126/', 'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/814286/000102660812000072/haln_10q33112.htm']","A land contract -- often described by other terminology listed below -- is a contract between the buyer and seller of real property in which the seller provides the buyer financing in the purchase, and the buyer repays the resulting loan in installments. Under a land contract, the seller retains the legal title to the property, while permitting the buyer to take possession of it for most purposes other than legal ownership. The sale price is typically paid in periodic installments, often with a balloon payment at the end to make the timelength of payments shorter than in the corresponding fully amortized loan (i.e., a loan without a final balloon payment). When the full purchase price has been paid including any interest, the seller is obligated to convey (to the buyer) legal title to the property. An initial down payment from the buyer to the seller is usually also required.","Metaglossary.com - Definitions for ""{term}"" About us Define Definitions for ""assumption"" Keywords: supposition, proposition, postulate, true, suppose Related Terms: Inference, Axiom , Deductive reasoning, Deduction, Infer, Postulate, Reasoning, Conjecture, Argumentation, Proof, Argument, Premise, Deductive, Theorem, Logic, Induction, Conclusion, Therefore, Validity, Contradiction, Reductio ad absurdum, Logical argument, Inductive, Apodictic, Evidence, Fact, Presupposition, Ratiocination, Inductive argument, Abduction, Counterexample, Truism, A priori, Sound argument, Modus tollens, Corollary, Indirect proof, Falsifiability, Inductive reasoning, Deductive argument, Paradox, Reason, A posteriori, Falsification, Begging the question, Argue, Invalid, Logical fallacy, Syllogism, Deduce The act of taking for granted, or supposing a thing without proof; supposition; unwarrantable claim. ftp.uga.edu The thing supposed; a postulate, or proposition assumed; a supposition. ftp.uga.edu The minor or second proposition in a categorical syllogism. ftp.uga.edu Acceptance of information as true without proof or demonstration. see also: False Assumption nwcg.gov Conditions that are expected to be true during the design and implementation phases of the project. npac.com ( as·SUMP·tion). The act of taking for granted, presupposing, or presuming a fact not in evidence or without proof. bibarch.com Factors that, for planning purposes, are considered to be true, real or certain. () ds.uillinois.edu A statement that has not been verified as true, but the project proceeds as if it were true. Any assumption can become a risk. carolla.com Assumptions are labels which are used to indicate that certain conditions are presumed to be true. They are often used to constrain the possible behaviour of a model. Because they describe neither structural nor behavioural aspects of a system, they belong to neither the structural building blocks nor the behavioural building blocks categories. hcs.science.uva.nl Something which is taken as true but is without proof. Assumptions are made during project planning and are used as a basis for estimating. It's a good idea to document all assumptions and to adjust plans and estimates as necessary if the assumptions turn out to be false. knowledgetrain.co.uk Thing accepted as true or as sure to happen, but not proved. spc.int Supposition of something to be a fact, whether proven or not pcrest.com a fact or statement (as a proposition, axiom, postulate, or notion) taken for granted webcom.com something is taken for granted as being true wells.entirety.ca Something that is understood to be true. novita.org.au (noun) Something taken for granted or accepted as true without proof; a supposition. nces.ed.gov Proposition or principle which you suppose is true or take for granted imls.gov In project management, a presumed truth for which the manager is unable or unwilling to verify its truthfulness. richardthayer.com an idea or concept that we take for granted; provisional suppositions. pearsoned.ca View 16 more results Keywords: mary, heaven, ascent, doctrine, soul Related Terms: Theotokos, Resurrection, Jesus christ, Marcionism, Atonement, Christian, Jesus, Ascension, Docetism, Arianism, Calvinist, Amillennialism, Christians, Gospel, Messiah, Antichrist, Rapture, Adoptionism, Kerygma, Elijah, Redemption, Son of god, Salvation, Savior, Christianity, Cohen, Arminianism, Theophany, Soteriology, Kingdom of god, Nestorianism, Nazarene, Fundamentalist, Sadducees, Christophany, Epiphany , Saints, Sadducee, Christ, Virgin birth, Arminian, Ezekiel, Matthew, God, Seraphim, Assumption of the blessed virgin mary, Cherubim, Reconciliation, Zechariah, Modalism A festival in honor of the ascent of the Virgin Mary into heaven. ftp.uga.edu Mary's ascent into heaven. This is an unusual theme in Ethiopian icons; the composition with the crescent moon support and surround of angels recalls foreign sources. africa.si.edu The doctrine that the Virgin Mary was bodily taken up into heaven at her death innvista.com the taking of the body and soul of Mary, by God, into glory. Catholic doctrine, apparently, does not state whether or not Mary died, but tradition holds that she died and was immediately afterward assumed into heaven both body and soul. carm.org The taking up of Mary (body and soul) into heaven. 4truth.net View 2 more results Keywords: liable, existing, seller, liability, take Related Terms: Assumption clause, Assumable mortgage, Assumption of mortgage, Assumption of a mortgage, Assumable, Assumable loan, Purchase-money mortgage, Assumability, Vendor take back mortgage, Vendor take