what is the origin of the name evans,"['https://www.thoughtco.com/evans-name-meaning-and-origin-1422501', 'http://www.name-doctor.com/name-carter-meaning-of-carter-5415.html', 'https://www.thoughtco.com/marshall-surname-meaning-and-origin-4098369', 'https://www.irelandroots.com/smith.htm', 'https://vampirediaries.fandom.com/wiki/Brian_Walters']","Evans is a surname of Welsh, and possibly Cornish, origin. Within Wales it is the fifth most common surname and is the tenth most common in England. Within the United States, it is ranked as the 48th-most common surname.","If you are a reader experiencing an access issue, please contact support@people.inc. To help us troubleshoot more quickly, you may include your IP address. You can find it by visiting icanhazip.com or whatismyip.com. If you would like to access our content for licensing, please contact contentlicensing@people.inc. [SEP_DOC] Carter Name Meaning & Origin | Name DoctorCarter: a male name of Latin origin meaning ""Carter is a family name, and also a given name"". It derives from the Latin word carrum > Char > chariot > CHARETIER > CART (E).In Latin tradition, Carter holds special significance as a masculine name representing Carter is a family name, and also a given name. This name has been cherished across generations, carrying with it the wisdom and heritage of Latin culture. Its enduring popularity speaks to its universal resonance.Quick Facts About CarterQuick facts about CarterOriginLatin, Old FrenchGenderBoy namePopularityModerately popularResearched by Name Doctor Linguistics TeamOur names are verified by professional linguists with expertise in etymology and cultural naming traditions.Last reviewed: November 2025MeaningCarter is a family name, and also a given name. Carter is an occupational name given to one who transports goods by cart or wagon. It is the 64th most common surname in the United Kingdom. Within the United States, it is ranked as the 40th-most common surname. This name derives from the Middle English “cart(e).” The Middle English term finally derives from the Old French “charetier,” meaning “charioteer,” from “chariot,” meaning “wagon,” which is the long-form of “Char,” meaning “car.” In turn, the name derives from the Latin “carrum,” meaning “four-wheel Gallic transport.”Etymology & DetailsName Rootcarrum > Char > chariot > CHARETIER > CART(E)How to Pronounce CarterCarter is pronounced /ˈkɑːrtər/(Latin)Step-by-step pronunciation guide:Break it into syllables: Car-terSay it like: ""KAHRTOOHR""Practice saying it naturally a few times✓Pronunciation verified by linguistic sourcesUsage & PopularityCarter enjoys international appeal, used in 4 different linguistic and cultural contexts including english, irish, scottish, and many others. This widespread adoption reflects its universal resonance.englishirishscottishwelshFrequently Asked Questions About CarterWhat does the name Carter mean?Carter means ""Carter is a family name, and also a given name. Carter is an occupational name given to one who transports goods by cart or wagon. It is the 64th most common surname in the United Kingdom. Within the United States, it is ranked as the 40th-most common surname. This name derives from the Middle English “cart(e).” The Middle English term finally derives from the Old French “charetier,” meaning “charioteer,” from “chariot,” meaning “wagon,” which is the long-form of “Char,” meaning “car.” In turn, the name derives from the Latin “carrum,” meaning “four-wheel Gallic transport.”"". The name comes from carrum > Char > chariot > CHARETIER > CART(E). This latin name has been passed down through generations, carrying its meaning across different cultures and time periods. Parents who choose Carter for boys often connect with the name's meaning of ""Carter is a family name, and also a given name. Carter is an occupational name given to one who transports goods by cart or wagon. It is the 64th most common surname in the United Kingdom. Within the United States, it is ranked as the 40th-most common surname. This name derives from the Middle English “cart(e).” The Middle English term finally derives from the Old French “charetier,” meaning “charioteer,” from “chariot,” meaning “wagon,” which is the long-form of “Char,” meaning “car.” In turn, the name derives from the Latin “carrum,” meaning “four-wheel Gallic transport.”"" as a quality they hope to see in their child. While not the most common choice, Carter's meaningful roots have given it staying power among parents looking for a name with substance.What is the origin of the name Carter?Carter has latin and old french origins. The name traces back to carrum > Char > chariot > CHARETIER > CART(E). From its latin roots, Carter spread to become common in english, irish, scottish, welsh. The latin meaning ""Carter is a family name, and also a given name. Carter is an occupational name given to one who transports goods by cart or wagon. It is the 64th most common surname in the United Kingdom. Within the United States, it is ranked as the 40th-most common surname. This name derives from the Middle English “cart(e).” The Middle English term finally derives from the Old French “charetier,” meaning “charioteer,” from “chariot,” meaning “wagon,” which is the long-form of “Char,” meaning “car.” In turn, the name derives from the Latin “carrum,” meaning “four-wheel Gallic transport.”"" reflects values important in the culture where this name first appeared.Is Carter a boy or girl name?Carter is a boy's name. The name has been used for boys across many generations, maintaining its masculine character through different eras and cultures. In latin tradition, Carter has historically been given to boys. The meaning ""Carter is a family name, and also a given name. Carter is an occupational name given to one who transports goods by cart or wagon. It is the 64th most common surname in the United Kingdo [SEP_DOC] If you are a reader experiencing an access issue, please contact support@people.