[79] After the evacuation of the French in Egypt in 1801, ‘Umar Makram, with the assistance of the jurists overthrew the French agent left behind. Instead of assuming power directly, the jurists offered the government to the Egyptianized Albanian Muḥammad ‘Alī. See Afaf Lutfi al-Sayyid Marsot, ‘The Ulama of Cairo in the Eighteenth and Nineteenth Century’ in Nikki Keddi (ed), Scholars, Saints, and Sufis (University of California Press, Berkeley 1972) 149-165, 162f.
[80] Marsot (n 79) 149.
[81] Marsot (n 79) 150. For an exhaustive study on the role of the ‘ulamā’ in legitimating rulers and rebellions through the use of their moral weight, see Abou El Fadl (n 15). On the social and political roles played by the ‘ulamā′, see Edward Mortimer, Faith and Power: The Politics of Islam (Vintage Books, New York 1982) 299-307; Malcolm H. Kerr, Islamic Reform: The Political and Legal Theories of Muḥammad ‘Abduh and Rashīd Riḍā (University of California Press, Berkeley and Los Angeles 1966) 196; Louis J. Cantori, ‘Religion and Politics in Egypt’ in Michael Curtis (ed), Religion and Politics in the Middle East (Westview Press, Boulder, Colorado 1981) 77-90.
[82] Marsot (n 79) 159.
[83] Daniel Crecelius, ‘Egyptian Ulama and Modernization’ in Keddi (n 79) 167-209, 168. Crecelius makes this point about the ‘ulamā′ of Egypt in the modern age. However, see Fouad Ajami, ‘In the Pharaoh's Shadow: Religion and Authority in Egypt’ in James Piscatori (ed), Islam in the Political Process (Cambridge University Press, London 1983) 18; Mortimer (n 81) 91, 95; Malise Ruthven, Islam in the World (Oxford University Press, Oxford 1984) 179. Of course, there are notable exceptions in the contemporary Islamic practice. Many clerics become prominent opponents of the present Muslim regimes, and suffer enormously for their troubles.
[84] On the idea of the praetorian state, see Amos Perlmutter, Egypt: The Praetorian State (Transaction Books, New Brunswick 1974). Also see Khaled Abou El Fadl, ‘Orphans of Modernity and the Clash of Civilisations.’ (2002) 4 (2) Global Dialogue 1, 16.
[85] I am simplifying this sophisticated doctrine in order to make a point. Muslim jurists engaged in lengthy attempts to differentiate between the two concepts of Sharī‘ah and fiqh. See Maḥmaṣānī (n 65) 21-24, 199f.; Weiss (n 72) 119-121; Abū Zahrah (n 61) 291; Muṣṭafā Zayd, al-Maṣlaḥah fī al-Tashrī‘ al-Islāmī wa Najm al-Dīn al-Ṭūfī (2nd edn Dār al-Fikr al-‘Arabī, Cairo 1964) 22; Yūsuf Ḥāmid al-‘Ālim, al-Maqāṣid al-‘Āmmah li-al-Sharī‘ah al-Islāmiyyah (International Institute of Islamic Thought, Herndon, Virginia 1991) 80; Muḥammad b. ‘Alī b. Muḥammad al-Shawkānī, Ṭalab al-‘Ilm wa Ṭabaqāt al-Muta‘allimīn: Adab al-Ṭalab wa Muntahā al-‘Arab (Dār al-Arqām, n.p. 1981) 145-151.
[86] The Arabic is ‘kull mujtahid muṣīb’ and ‘li-kull mujtahid naṣīb’. See Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad b. ‘Alī b. al-Ṭayyib al-Baṣrī, al-Mu‘tamad fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Dār al-Kutub al-‘Ilmiyyah, Beirut 1983) 2:370-372; ‘Alā’ al-Dīn ‘Abd al-‘Azīz b. Aḥmadī al-Bukhārī (d. 730/1329) in Muḥammad al-Mu‘taṣim bi-’llāh al-Baghdādī (ed), Kashf al-Asrār ‘an Uṣūl Fakhr al-Islām al-Bazdāwī (3rd edn Dār al-Kitāb al-‘Arabī, Beirut 1997) 4:30-55; Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī (n 61) 2:363-367; Abū al-Ma‘ālī ‘Abd al-Malik b. ‘Abd Allāh b. Yūsuf al-Juwaynī, Kitāb al-Ijtihād min Kitāb al-Talkhīṣ (Dār al-Qalam, Damascus 1987) 26-32; Abū Muḥammad ‘Alī b. Aḥmad b. Sa‘īd b. Ḥazm, al-Iḥkām fī Uṣūl al-Aḥkām (Dār al-Ḥadīṯ, Cairo 1984) 5:68-81, 8:589-592; Muḥammad b. Aḥmad b. ‘Abd al-‘Azīz b. ‘Alī al-Fatūḥī Ibn al-Najjār (d. 972/1564) in Muḥammad al-Zuḥaylī and Nazīr Ḥammād (eds), Sharḥ al-Kawkab al-Munīr al-Musammā Mukhtaṣar al-Taḥrīr aw al-Mukhtabar al-Mubtakar Sharḥ al-Mukhtaṣar fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Maktabat al-‘Ubaykān, Riyadh 1993) 4:488-492; Abū Bakr Aḥmad b. ‘Alī b. Ṯābit al-Khaṭīb al-Baghdādī, Kitāb al-Faqīh wa al-Mutafaqqih wa Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Zakariyyā ‘Alī Yūsuf, Cairo 1977) 245-250; Abū al-Ṯanā’ Maḥmūd b. Zayd al-Lāmishī (d. 539/114) in ‘Abd al-Majīd Turkī (ed), Kitāb fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Dār al-Gharb al-Islāmī, Beirut 1995) 201f.; al-Qarāfī (n 61) 438-441; Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī (n 61) 6:29-36; Muḥammad b. ‘Alī b. Muḥammad al-Shawkānī, Irshād al-Fuḥūl ilā Taḥqīq al-Ḥaqq min ‘Ilm al-Uṣūl (Dār al-Kutub al-’Ilmiyyah, Beirut n.d.) 383-389; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm b. ‘Alī b. Yūsuf al-Fayrūzābādī al-Shīrāzī (d. 476/1083) in ‘Abd al-Majīd Turkī (ed), Sharḥ al-Lum’ah (Dār al-Gharb al-Islāmī, Beirut 1988) 2:1043-1071; idem in Muḥammad Ḥasan Haytū (ed), al-Tabṣirah fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Dār al-Fikr, Damascus 1980) 496-508. In this context, Muslim jurists also debated a report attributed to the Prophet in which he says, ‘whoever performs ijtihād and is correct will be rewarded twice and whoever is wrong will be rewarded once.’ See Jalāl al-Dīn ‘Abd al-Raḥmān b. Abī Bakr al-Suyūṭī in ‘Abd al-Qayyūm Muḥammad Shafī‘ al-Basṭawī (ed), Ikhtilāf al-Madhāhib (Dār al-I‘tiṣām, Cairo 1983 C.E./1404 A.H.) 38. Ibn Ḥazm (n 86) 5:73f., 8:591; idem in Muḥammad Ṣubḥī Ḥasan Ḥallāq (ed), al-Nubadh fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh al-Ẓāhirī (Dār Ibn Ḥazm, Beirut 1993) 119f.; Abū al-Ḥasan ‘Alī b. ‘Umar Ibn al-Qaṣṣār (d. 397/1007) in Muḥammad b. al-Ḥusayn al-Sulaymānī (ed), al-Muqaddimah fī al-Uṣūl (Dār al-Gharb al-Islāmī, Beirut 1996) 114f.; Maḥfūẓ b. Aḥmad b. al-Ḥasan Abū al-Khaṭṭāb al-Kalūzānī, (d. 510/1116) in Muḥammad b. ‘Alī b. Ibrāhīm (ed), al-Tamhīd fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Markaz al-Baḥṯ al-‘Ilmī wa Iḥyā’ al-Turāṯ al-Islāmī, Mecca 1985) 4:317f.; al-Qarāfī (n 61) 440; Abū ‘Abd Allāh Muḥammad b. Idrīs al-Shāfi‘ī, (d. 204/820) in Aḥmad Muḥammad Shākir (ed), al-Risālah (Dār al-Fikr, n.p. n.d.) 49 4; al-Shīrāzī (n 86) al-Tabṣirah, 499; Muḥammad b. ‘Abd al-Ḥamīd al-Asmandī (d. 552/1157) in Muḥammad Zakī ‘Abd al-Barr (ed), Badhl al-Naẓar fī al-Uṣūl (Maktabat Dār al-Turāṯ, Cairo 1992) 702f.
[87] For discussions of the two schools, see al-Bukhārī (n 86) 4:18; Abū Ḥāmid Muḥammad b. Muḥammad al-Ghazālī, al-Mankhūl min Ta‘līqāt al-Uṣūl (Dār al-Fikr, Damascus 1980) 455; idem (n 61) 2:550f.; Fakhr al-Dīn Muḥammad b. ‘Umar b. al-Ḥusayn al-Rāzī, al-Maḥṣūl fī ‘Ilm Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Dār al-Kutub al-‘Ilmiyyah, Beirut 1988) 2:500-508; al-Qarāfī (n 61) 438; al-Zuḥaylī (n 61) 638-655; Ḥasab Allāh (n 62) 82f.; Badrān (n 62) 474.
[88] Al-Juwaynī (n 86) 50f.
[89] Al-Juwaynī (n 86) 61.
[90] Sayf al-Dīn Abū al-Ḥasan ‘Alī b. Abī ‘Alī b. Muḥammad al-Āmidī (d. 630/1233) in ‘Abd al-Razzāq ‘Afīfī (ed), al-Iḥkām fī Uṣūl al-Aḥkām (2nd edn al-Maktab al-Islāmī, Beirut 1981 C.E./1402 A.H.) 4:183; Jamāl al-Dīn Abī Muḥammad ‘Abd al-Raḥīm b. al-Ḥasan al-Asnawī, al-Tamhīd fī Takhrīj al-Furū‘ ‘alā al-Uṣūl (3rd edn Mu’assasat al-Risālah, Beirut 1984) 531-534; Muḥammad b. al-Ḥasan al-Badakhshī, Sharḥ al-Badakhshī Manāhij al-‘Uqūl ma‘a Sharḥ al-Asnawī Nihāyat al-Sūl (Dār al-Kutub al-‘Ilmiyyah, Beirut 1984) 3:275-281; Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī (n 61) 2:375-378; al-Juwaynī (n 86) 41; Abū al-Ṯanā’ Maḥmūd b. Zayd al-Lāmishī in ‘Abd al-Majīd Turkī (ed), Kitāb fī Uṣūl al-Fiqh (Dār al-Gharb al-Islāmī, Beirut 1995) 202f.; al-Qarāfī (n 61) 440; Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī (n 61) 6:34f., 6:43-50; Sha’bān (n 61) 418f.; Badrān, (n 62) 474; al-Zuḥaylī (n 61) 643.
[91] I deal with these two schools of thought more extensively elsewhere, see Khaled Abou El Fadl, Speaking in God’s Name: Islamic Law, Authority and Women (Oneworld Publications, Oxford 2001).
[92] I am ignoring in this context the role of ijmā‘ (consensus) because of the complexity of the subject. Some modern Muslims have argued that the doctrine of consensus is the normative equivalent of majority rule. I think this is a gross oversimplification, and, at any case, majority rule, as explained, is not the same as constitutionalism.
[93] Contemporary Islamic discourses suffer from a certain amount of hypocrisy in this regard. Often, Muslims confront an existential crisis if the enforced, so-called Islamic laws result in social suffering and misery. In order to solve this crisis Muslims will often claim that there has been a failure in the circumstances of implementation. This indulgence in unnecessary apologetics could be avoided if the problematic idea of Sharī‘ah state law is abandoned.
[94] This proposal is nonsense unless the ‘ulamā’ regain their institutional and moral independence."
what is the function of hard disk drive,"['https://www.leedsdatarecovery.co.uk/devices/hard-drive-recovery/', 'http://balonchik-xxx.info/category/internet-services/', 'https://moite-mebeli.info/fullerton-square-platter.htm', 'http://aidfilerecovery.com/HGST-hard-drive-file-recovery.htm', 'http://info-info-info-info-info.info/?page_id=44111', 'http://portisaac.info/?page_id=33359', 'http://gomino.info/?option=com_content&view=article&id=48448']","A hard disk drive (HDD), hard disk, hard drive or fixed disk is an electromechanical data storage device that uses magnetic storage to store and retrieve digital information using one or more rigid rapidly rotating disks (platters) coated with magnetic material. The platters are paired with magnetic heads, usually arranged on a moving actuator arm, which read and write data to the platter surfaces. Data is accessed in a random-access manner, meaning that individual blocks of data can be stored or retrieved in any order and not only sequentially. HDDs are a type of non-volatile storage, retaining stored data even when powered off.","Hard Drive Data Recovery Service – Leeds Data Recovery
With 25 years of experience, Leeds Data Recovery specialises in recovering data from all leading hard drive manufacturers and models. We support a wide range of hard drive types, including HDD, SSD, SSHD, NVMe, SATA, SAS, SCSI, IDE, 3.5″, 2.5″, M.2, and U.2 drives. Our expert team can resolve various issues, from physical damage to logical errors, ensuring your valuable data is retrieved efficiently and securely.
Top Hard Drive Manufacturers We Support
We recover data from the top 10 selling hard drive brands in the UK and Ireland, including:
- Toshiba
- Western Digital (WD)
- Seagate
- Bipra
- HGST
- Samsung
- Crucial
- Intel
- SanDisk
- Kingston
Supported Hard Drive Types
- HDD (Hard Disk Drives): 3.5″ desktop drives, 2.5″ laptop drives, enterprise-grade drives.
- SSD (Solid-State Drives): SATA SSDs, M.2 NVMe SSDs, PCIe SSDs, and U.2 SSDs.
- SSHD (Hybrid Drives): Combining HDD storage with SSD speed.
- Enterprise Drives: SAS, SCSI, and RAID-configured drives.
Common Hard Drive Issues We Recover From
1. Physical Damage
- Examples: Damaged platters, read/write heads, or connectors.
- Our Solution: HDD repairs and advanced recovery techniques.
2. Logical Errors
- Examples: File system corruption, partition table errors, or accidental formatting.
- Our Solution: Logical data recovery using specialised software.
3. Firmware Failures
- Examples: Corrupted firmware causing the drive to become inaccessible.
- Our Solution: Firmware restoration or bypass.
4. Bad Sectors
- Examples: Portions of the drive become unreadable due to wear and tear.
- Solution: Data extracted from healthy sectors.
5. Electrical Failures
- Examples: Power surges damaging the PCB (Printed Circuit Board).
- Solution: PCB repair or replacement.
6. RAID Failures
- Examples: Degraded RAID arrays, failed rebuilds, or multiple drive failures.
- Solution: Reconstruction of RAID configurations and data recovery.
7. Human Error
- Examples: Accidental deletion, formatting, or reconfiguration.
- Solution: Advanced recovery techniques to retrieve lost files.
8. Software and Malware Issues
- Examples: Virus attacks, ransomware, or OS-level corruption.
- Solution: Data recovery combined with malware removal.
9. Overheating
- Examples: Prolonged use causing internal damage to components.
- Solution: Repairs and recovery from damaged drives.
10. Drive Not Recognised
- Examples: BIOS or operating system fails to detect the drive.
- Solution: Diagnosis and repair of underlying hardware or software issues.
11. Knock or Fall
- Examples: Physical impact causing internal misalignment or component damage.
- Solution: Recovery and mechanical repairs.
12. Water Damage
- Examples: Drives exposed to liquids, causing short circuits or corrosion.
- Solution: Professional recovery to minimise further damage.
By Storage Technology – We recover all HDD types:
1. Hard Disk Drive (HDD)
- Description: Uses spinning magnetic platters and a read/write head to store data.
- Applications: Desktop computers, laptops, servers, external storage.
- Advantages: Cost-effective for large storage capacities.
- Disadvantages: Slower speed and more prone to mechanical failures.
2. Solid-State Drive (SSD)
- Description: Uses NAND flash memory for fast, reliable data storage with no moving parts.
- Applications: Laptops, desktops, enterprise storage, mobile devices.
- Advantages: Faster data access, low power consumption, more durable.
- Disadvantages: Higher cost per GB compared to HDDs.
3. Hybrid Drive (SSHD)
- Description: Combines SSD speed with HDD capacity. Frequently accessed data is stored on the SSD portion, while the rest is stored on the HDD.
- Applications: Consumer desktops and laptops.
- Advantages: Balances speed and capacity at a moderate cost.
- Disadvantages: Performance still slower than full SSDs.
4. NVMe (Non-Volatile Memory Express) Drives
- Description: Uses PCIe interface for extremely fast data access, often in M.2 or U.2 form factors.
- Applications: High-performance laptops, desktops, gaming systems, enterprise servers.
- Advantages: High-speed data transfer and low latency.
- Disadvantages: More expensive than SATA SSDs.
By Connection Interface
1. SATA (Serial ATA) Drives
- Description: Common interface for both HDDs and SSDs.
- Applications: Most consumer-grade computers and storage systems.
- Advantages: Widely compatible, affordable.
- Disadvantages: Slower than NVMe.
2. SAS (Serial Attached SCSI) Drives
- Description: High-speed interface mainly used in enterprise environments.
- Applications: Servers, data centers, and professional workstations.
- Advantages: Reliable, supports simultaneous data access.
- Disadvantages: More expensive and complex than SATA.
3. SCSI (Small Computer System Interface) Drives
- Description: An older interface, now largely replaced by SAS.
- Applications: Legacy systems and enterprise storage.
- Advantages: Robust and durable for heavy workloads.
- Disadvantages: Outdated and slower compared to modern interfaces.
4. IDE (Integrated Drive Electronics) Drives
- Description: An older interface standard for HDDs.
- Applications: Legacy desktop and laptop computers.
- Advantages: Compatible with older systems.
- Disadvantages: Limited speed and capacity.
By Form Factor
1. 3.5-Inch Drives
- Description: Standard size for desktop and server HDDs.
- Applications: Desktop PCs, servers, and external storage enclosures.
- Advantages: Higher capacity.
- Disadvantages: Larger physical size, less portable.
2. 2.5-Inch Drives
- Description: Smaller form factor used in laptops and portable external drives.
- Applications: Laptops, gaming consoles, and external enclosures.
- Advantages: Compact and lightweight.
- Disadvantages: Typically lower capacity than 3.5-inch drives.
3. M.2 Drives
- Description: Compact SSDs designed for high-speed storage.
- Applications: Ultrabooks, gaming PCs, and enterprise servers.
- Advantages: Extremely fast and space-saving.
- Disadvantages: Compatibility depends on the system.
4. U.2 Drives
- Description: Enterprise-grade SSDs with NVMe interface.
- Applications: High-performance servers and data centers.
- Advantages: High speed and reliability.
- Disadvantages: Requires specific hardware compatibility.
Specialised Drive Types
1. External Hard Drives
- Description: Enclosed drives that connect via USB, Thunderbolt, or eSATA.
- Applications: Backup, portable storage.
- Advantages: Portable and easy to use.
- Disadvantages: Slower than internal drives.
2. Enterprise Drives
- Description: High-performance drives built for data centers and servers.
- Applications: Cloud storage, enterprise-grade applications.
- Advantages: High reliability, better durability.
- Disadvantages: Expensive.
3. RAID Drives
- Description: Drives used in RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks) configurations for redundancy and performance.
- Applications: Enterprise storage, NAS devices.
- Advantages: Data redundancy, higher performance.
- Disadvantages: Requires RAID setup and management.
Why Choose Leeds Data Recovery?
- Extensive Experience: Over two decades of successful data recovery from all drive types and brands.
- Comprehensive Support: Capable of handling physical, logical, and environmental issues.
- Cutting-Edge Technology: Advanced tools for recovering data from even severely damaged drives.
- Secure Process: Adherence to strict data privacy and confidentiality protocols.
- No-Recovery, No-Fee Guarantee: Transparent pricing and no hidden charges.
Our Data Recovery Process
