where did the worm in tequila come from,"['https://margaritatexas.com/news/using-mezcal-in-margaritas/', 'https://vinepair.com/spirits-101/intro-mezcal-guide/', 'https://thegadabout.co.uk/mezcal', 'https://socalrestaurantshow.com/tag/oaxaca/', 'https://bastondelrey.com/2017/02/04/interesting-facts-tequila/', 'http://beachcomberdestin.com/2016/04/21/tequila-plus-mystery-equals-mezcal/', 'https://blog.dinersclub.com/2018/10/10/chefs-maria-elena-lugo-zermeno-and-gerardo-vazquez-lugo-qa/']","Contrary to the popular belief of many, a true Tequila does not contain a worm. It is only in Tequila's brother Mezcal.","Using Mezcal in Margaritas – Margarita Texas Search Close Search Using Mezcal in Margaritas By Margarita TexasNews No Comments Margaritaville Frozen Concoction Maker Review Best Margaritas in Dallas We haven’t touched on mezcal on this website yet but more and more you are seeing new brands pop-up and restaurants using the spirit in margaritas. If you are looking to add a twist to your drink, we recommend experimenting with mezcal. If you do not have any experience with it, then let’s start at the beginning. What is Mezcal? Mezcal is a distilled alcoholic beverage made from the maguey plant (a form of agave — which is the plant that is used to make tequila) native to Mexico. The word mezcal comes from the words Nahuatl mexcalli which means “oven-cooked agave”. Mezcal has what is generally described as a smoky flavor which is gets during the production process. The hearts of the agave plants, piñas, are cooked in pits in the ground slowly. Other popular descriptions of the taste includes spicy, ash, wood and fire. It is unclear whether distilled drinks were produced in Mexico before the Spanish Conquest. The Spaniards were introduced to native fermented drinks such as pulque, also made from the maguey plant. Soon, the conquistadors began experimenting with the maguey plant to find a way to make a distillable fermented mash. The result was mezcal. Today, mezcal is still made from the heart of the maguey plant, called the piña, much the same way it was 200 years ago, in most places. In Mexico, mezcal is generally consumed straight and has a strong smoky flavor. Though mezcal is not as popular as tequila (a mezcal made specifically from the blue agave in select regions of the country), Mexico does export the product, mostly to Japan and the United States, and exports are growing. In Mexico, mezcal is generally drunk straight, not mixed in a cocktail. Mezcal is generally not mixed with any other liquids, but is often accompanied with sliced oranges sprinkled with a mixture of ground fried larvae, ground chili peppers, and salt called sal de gusano, which literally translates as “worm salt”. The Worm Contrary to the popular belief, tequila does not contain a worm. Anyone making that assumption may be confused with mezcal which Chef Rick Bayless describes as the “rustic cousin of tequila.” So, don’t be shocked if you pick up a bottle of mezcal and find one of these tiny creatures. A mezcal worm is an insect larva found in a few types of mezcal produced in Oaxaca, Mexico. The larva is usually either a gusano rojo (“red worm”) or a chinicuil (“maguey worm”). Although the custom is relatively recent, larvae are used frequently by several brands of mezcal to give flavor to the drink. A whole larva is deposited in the bottle, normally after having previously been cured in pure alcohol. Popular Mezcal Brands Ilegal Mezcal Del Maguey Vida Wahaka Joven Espadin Mezcal Pierde Almas Espadín Mezcal Margarita Recipe 1 1/2 oz Mezcal 3/4 oz orange liqueur 1/2 oz lime  [SEP_DOC] The Complete Mezcal Guide (UPDATED 2026) Table Of Contents Mezcal 101 Popular Mezcal Brands Mezcal Reviews Mezcal Buying Guides Mezcal Infographics Modern Mezcal Cocktails Recent Mezcal Articles Popular Mezcal Brands 10 Things You Should Know About Madre Mezcal 8 Things You Should Know About Clase Azul 8 Things You Should Know About Casamigos 10 Things You Should Know About Ilegal Mezcal 8 Things You Should Know About Del Maguey Mezcal Recent Mezcal Reviews Tlamati Destilado de Agave, Tequilana Weber Cinco Sentidos Arroqueño Tío Pedro NETA Espadin-Bicuixe Heriberto García Sánchez Los Perdidos Tepeztate Aged 4 Months in Cognac Cask Cumbrita de la Sierra Lechuguilla & Chawi Ensamble Gusto Histórico Madrecuixe Victor Ramos Palenqueros Madrecuishe Valente Garcia Mina Real Mezcal Blanco Doce Mezcal See All Mezcal Reviews The Best Bottles Of Mezcal To Buy The 7 Best Spirits Under $50 to Gift This Holiday The 20 Best Mezcals for 2025 The 7 Best Mezcals to Gift This Holiday (2024) The 16 Best Large-Format Bottled Cocktails The 15 Best Mezcals for 2024 The 50 Best Spirits of 2023 The 7 Best Mezcals to Gift This Holiday (2023) The 25 Best Mezcals for 2023 The 7 Best Mezcals to Gift This Holiday (2022) The 9 Best Mezcals for Cocktails 12 of the Best Celebrity Spirits, Tasted and Ranked (2020) Six of the Best Mezcals Under $50, Tasted and Ranked Mezcal Infographics & Maps Every Major Spirit Category and Its Base Ingredient [Infographic] The Essential Shot Guide [Infographic] A Complete Guide to Bitters and the Cocktails to Make With Them [Infographic] Understanding the Differences Between Tequila and Mezcal (Infographic) Modern Mezcal Cocktails The Smoke, Spice, and Everything Nice Recipe The Berry-Mezcal Colada Recipe The Mexicano Recipe Strawberry Mezcal Flip Recipe The Pineapple Mezcal Sour Recipe The Smoky Orange And Thyme Margarita Recipe Recent Articles About Mezcal Is Mole Pechuga Mezcal About to Have Its Moment? The Flex Spirit Bottles of 2026, According to 11 Bartenders We Asked 10 Bartenders: What’s the Best ‘Panic-Order’ Cocktail? We Asked 15 Bartenders: What’s the Best Mezcal for Beginners? The Key to Dolores’s White Negroni? Tepache. What Does ‘Reading the Pearls’ Mean in Mezcal Production? The 5 Key Things to Look for When Buying Mezcal The Historic Mezcal Harvesting Technique Disappearing in the Modern Era Espadín: The Surprising