the establishment clause of the first amendment is often referred to as the,"['http://www.morganhuneke.com/blog/the-separation-of-church-and-state', 'https://caselaw.findlaw.com/us-supreme-court/530/290.html', 'https://www.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/faith-and-politics-step-out/article772026/', 'https://swap.stanford.edu/20090125041326/http:/www.firstamendmentcenter.org/news.aspx?id%3D16832', 'https://www.borntowin.net/audio/religion-and-politics/', 'https://www.hwalibrary.com/cgi-bin/get/hwa.cgi?action=getmagazine&InfoID=1352720438', 'http://www.seekfind.net/SupremeCourt.html']","In United States law, the Establishment Clause of the First Amendment to the United States Constitution, together with that Amendment's Free Exercise Clause, form the constitutional right of freedom of religion. The relevant constitutional text is: ""Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof ..."".","SANTA FE INDEPENDENT SCHOOL DIST. v. DOE, 530 U.S. 290 (2000) | FindLaw SANTA FE INDEPENDENT SCHOOL DISTRICT v. DOE, individually and as next friend for her minor children, et al. 530 U.S. 290 (2000) United States Supreme Court SANTA FE INDEPENDENT SCHOOL DISTRICT v. DOE, individually and as next friend for her minor children, et al. (2000) No. 99-62 Argued: March 29, 2000 Decided: June 19, 2000 Prior to 1995, a student elected as Santa Fe High School's student council chaplain delivered a prayer over the public address system before each home varsity football game. Respondents, Mormon and Catholic students or alumni and their mothers, filed a suit challenging this practice and others under the Establishment Clause of the First Amendment. While the suit was pending, petitioner school district (District) adopted a different policy, which authorizes two student elections, the first to determine whether ""invocations"" should be delivered at games, and the second to select the spokesperson to deliver them. After the students held elections authorizing such prayers and selecting a spokesperson, the District Court entered an order modifying the policy to permit only nonsectarian, nonproselytizing prayer. The Fifth Circuit held that, even as modified by the District Court, the football prayer policy was invalid. Held: The District's policy permitting student-led, student-initiated prayer at football games violates the Establishment Clause. Pp. 9-26. (a) The Court's analysis is guided by the principles endorsed in Lee v. Weisman, 505 U. S. 577. There, in concluding that a prayer delivered by a rabbi at a graduation ceremony violated the Establishment Clause, the Court held that, at a minimum, the Constitution guarantees that government may not coerce anyone to support or participate in religion or its exercise, or otherwise act in a way that establishes a state religion or religious faith, or tends to do so, id., at 587. The District argues unpersuasively that these principles are inapplicable because the policy's messages are private student speech, not public speech. The delivery of a message such as the invocation here--on school property, at school-sponsored events, over the school's public address system, by a speaker representing the student body, under the supervision of school faculty, and pursuant to a school policy that explicitly and implicitly encourages public prayer--is not properly characterized as ""private"" speech. Although the District relies heavily on this Court's cases addressing public forums, e.g., Rosenberger v. Rector and Visitors of Univ. of Va., 515 U. S. 819, it is clear that the District's pregame ceremony is not the type of forum discussed in such cases. The District simply does not evince an intent to open its ceremony to indiscriminate use by the student body generally, see, e.g., Hazelwood School Dist. v. Kuhlmeier, 484 U. S. 260, 270, but, rather, allows only one student, the same student for the entire season, to give the"
when did the jurassic period start and end,"['https://wikimili.com/en/Cretaceous', 'https://wikivisually.com/wiki/Jurassic', 'https://paleontologyworld.com/exploring-prehistoric-life-prehistoric-flora-fauna/jurassic', 'https://wikivisually.com/wiki/Australopithecus_afarensis', 'https://inspiringnsw.org.au/2017/09/04/jurassic-garden-app/', 'http://terranchurch.org/ultima-ratio/primeval/primeval-14/', 'http://www.geologypage.com/2014/04/jurassic-period.html']","The Jurassic (/ dʒ ʊəˈr æsɪk /; from Jura Mountains) was a geologic period and system that spanned 56 million years from the end of the Triassic Period 201.3 million years ago (Mya) to the beginning of the Cretaceous Period 145 Mya. The Jurassic constitutes the middle period of the Mesozoic Era, also known as the Age of Reptiles. The start of the period was marked by the major Triassic -- Jurassic extinction event. Two other extinction events occurred during the period: the Pliensbachian / Toarcian event in the Early Jurassic, and the Tithonian event at the end; however, neither event ranks among the ""Big Five"" mass extinctions.","Jurassic - WikiVisually WikiVisually Infinite photos and videos for every Wiki article · Find something interesting to watch in seconds WikiVisually Infinite photos and videos for every Wiki article · Find something interesting to watch in seconds Ancient Marvels Celebrities Largest Empires History by Country Sports Kings of France Wars and Battles World Banknotes Animals British Monarchs Rare Coins Best Campuses Great Museums Largest Palaces Presidents Recovered Treasures Crown Jewels Richest US Counties Wonders of Nature Great Artists Orders and Medals Countries of the World Great Cities Supercars Tallest Buildings Famous Castles more top lists History Jurassic Videos Photos The Jurassic is a geologic period and stratigraphic system that spanned from the end of the Triassic Period 201.4 million years ago (Mya) to the beginning of the Cretaceous Period, approximately 145 Mya. The Jurassic constitutes the middle period of the Mesozoic Era and is named after the Jura Mountains, where limestone strata from the period were first identified. Portrait of Alexandre Brongniart, who coined the term ""Jurassic"" Folded Lower Jurassic limestone layers of the Doldenhorn nappe at Gasteretal, Switzerland Middle Jurassic strata in Neuquén Province, Argentina Tidwell Member of the Morrison Formation (Upper Jurassic), Colorado Triassic Videos Photos The Triassic is a geologic period and system which spans 50.5 million years from the end of the Permian Period 251.902 million years ago (Mya), to the beginning of the Jurassic Period 201.4 Mya. The Triassic is the first and shortest period of the Mesozoic Era. Both the start and end of the period are marked by major extinction events. The Triassic Period is subdivided into three epochs: Early Triassic, Middle Triassic and Late Triassic. Early Triassic sandstone (Buntsandstein) near Stadtroda, Germany Middle Triassic Muschelkalk (shell-bearing limestone) near Dörzbach, Germany Late Triassic Steigerwald Formation and overlying Hassberge Formation in Schönbuch, Germany Sydney, Australia lies on Triassic shales and sandstones. Almost all of the exposed rocks around Sydney belong to the Triassic Sydney sandstone."
name of the four presidents on mount rushmore,"['http://www.wapstt.com/2018/11/01/mount-rushmore/', 'https://laninga.me/', 'http://www.1000wondersoftheworld.com/details.php?id=5555', 'http://www.lostmonuments.com/mount-rushmore.html', 'https://this-day-in-history.net/tag/United_States/', 'https://simonandsandra.com/2013/07/15/day-62-the-black-hills-of-dakota/', 'http://secludedlandandhomeforsale.com/mount-rushmore/']","Mount Rushmore National Memorial is a sculpture carved into the granite face of Mount Rushmore, a batholith in the Black Hills in Keystone, South Dakota, United States. Sculptor Gutzon Borglum created the sculpture's design and oversaw the project's execution from 1927 to 1941 with the help of his son, Lincoln Borglum. Mount Rushmore features 60-foot (18 m) sculptures of the heads of four United States presidents: George Washington (1732 -- 1799), Thomas Jefferson (1743 -- 1826), Theodore Roosevelt (1858 -- 1919), and Abraham Lincoln (1809 -- 1865). The memorial park covers 1,278.45 acres (2.00 sq mi; 5.17 km) and is 5,725 feet (1,745 m) above sea level.",name of the four presidents on mount rushmore
importance of right to information act in india,"['http://worldheritage.org/articles/eng/Right_to_Information_Act', 'http://bskcollegebarharwa.com/rti.html', 'http://utrpurd.borec.cz/461-essay-on-right-to-information-act-and-its-fallout-3.php', 'http://www.onevasai.com/viewcontent/3447/rti-form-download', 'https://samples.edusson.com/the-government-of-india/', 'http://www.chikkaballapurapolice.in/rights-to-information-act/', 'http://www.thiruvananthapuram.info/right-to-information-act.php']","Right to Information (RTI) is an Act of the Parliament of India to provide for setting out the practical regime of the right to information for citizens and replaces the erstwhile Freedom of information Act, 2002. Under the provisions of the Act, any citizen of India may request information from a ""public authority"" (a body of Government or ""instrumentality of State"") which is required to reply expeditiously or within thirty days. The Act also requires every public authority to computerise their records for wide dissemination and to proactively certain categories of information so that the citizens need minimum recourse to request for information formally.","World Heritage Encyclopedia About Encyclopedia Contact Sign in / Register Sign in with your eLibrary Card close Find Discovery Comprehensive Collection of Scholarly Articles Millions of reviewed and edited articles in all academic interests Read online or download and save as eDocuments All articles are Print-on-Demand ready Largest encyclopedia The largest and most comprehensive Encyclopedia ever compiled A combined aggregation of about 1,000 article database, over 30,000,000 articles in total Perfect resource Beautifully presented and an easy to use interface Share the joy of discovery together Register today your eLibrary Card at no cost to you World Heritage Encyclopedia Where the World goes to explore... Great Artwork Science/Astronomy Tech/Engineering Biology/Medicine Philosophy/Thinkers Architecture/Civil Planning Sports/Athletes Entertainers/Performers Among the Sierra Nevada Mountains Art Mount Corcoran Art The Creation of Adam Art Mona Lisa Art The Starry Night Art Dogs Playing Poker Art The Garden of Earthly Delights Art The Son of Man Art Guernica (Picasso) Art The Storm on the Sea of Galilee Art Girl with a Pearl Earring Art Magnolia and Erect Rock Art NGC 6302 Science, Astronomy Pillars of Creation Science, Astronomy NGC 6357 Science, Astronomy Mystic Mountain Science, Astronomy Solar Flare Science, Astronomy Hubble Space Telescope Science, Astronomy Crab Nebula Science, Astronomy Black hole Science, Astronomy Parkes Observatory Science, Astronomy Dark nebula Science, Astronomy Solar flare Science, Astronomy Milky Way Science, Astronomy Stationary steam engine Tech, Engineering International Space Station Tech, Engineering Turbine Tech, Engineering Kismet (robot) Tech, Engineering National Ignition Facility Tech, Engineering Printing press Tech, Engineering Intel 4004 Tech, Engineering Ford Model T Tech, Engineering Wright Flyer Tech, Engineering Rocketdyne F-1 Tech, Engineering Soyuz TMA-14M Tech, Engineering Shinkansen Tech, Engineering Large Hadron Collider Tech, Engineering Escherichia coli Biology, Medicine Vibrio cholerae Biology, Medicine Polio vaccine Biology, Medicine Cell (biology) Biology, Medicine Lymphocyte Biology, Medicine Cough medicine Biology, Medicine Prokaryote Biology, Medicine Mitochondrion Biology, Medicine Unicellular organism Biology, Medicine Ogataea polymorpha Biology, Medicine Penicillin Biology, Medicine Ibuprofen Biology, Medicine Mahatma Gandhi Philosophy, Thinkers Sun Tzu Philosophy, Thinkers Aristotle Philosophy, Thinkers Gautama Buddha Philosophy, Thinkers Confucius Philosophy, Thinkers Averroes Philosophy, Thinkers Friedrich Nietzsche Philosophy, Thinkers Immanuel Kant Philosophy, Thinkers Plato Philosophy, Thinkers Socrates Philosophy, Thinkers Sigmund Freud Philosophy, Thinkers Thomas Hobbes Philosophy, Thinkers Florence Cathedral