when did the country of kenya come into existence,"['http://africacelebritytours.com/indian-ocean-islands-destinations.php', 'http://footwww.academickids.com/encyclopedia/index.php/Kenya', 'https://www.ngyab.com/2018/01/kenya-gain-independence/', 'https://q2travel.co.za/countries/kenya/', 'https://www.thoughtco.com/brief-history-of-kenya-44232', 'https://informationcradle.com/kenya/history-of-kenya/', 'http://www.kenyatravelideas.com/kenya-history.html']","The first direct elections for native Kenyans to the Legislative Council took place in 1957. Despite British hopes of handing power to ""moderate"" local rivals, it was the Kenya African National Union (KANU) of Jomo Kenyatta that formed a government. The Colony of Kenya and the Protectorate of Kenya each came to an end on 12 December 1963 with independence being conferred on all of Kenya. The United Kingdom ceded sovereignty over the Colony of Kenya. The Sultan of Zanzibar agreed that simultaneous with independence for the Colony of Kenya, the Sultan would cease to have sovereignty over the Protectorate of Kenya so that all of Kenya would be one sovereign, independent state. In this way, Kenya became an independent country under the Kenya Independence Act 1963 of the United Kingdom. Exactly 12 months later on 12 December 1964, Kenya became a republic under the name ""Republic of Kenya"".","404 Not Found Not Found The requested URL was not found on this server. Additionally, a 404 Not Found error was encountered while trying to use an ErrorDocument to handle the request."
what is the difference between a word and a morpheme,"['https://doowansnewsandevents.wordpress.com/2013/04/20/words/', 'https://wiki.thingsandstuff.org/Language', 'http://wikipage.com.ua/1x1176.html', 'https://www.education.com/weekly-boost/?week=24&grade=fourth-grade&type=parent&section=word-study', 'https://grammarofsanskrit.wordpress.com/2016/03/', 'http://sanjaymeena.io/tech/nlp/introduction-to-linguistics/', 'https://www.mediameasurement.com/mm/linguistic-diversity-in-social-listening-market-research/']","A morpheme is the smallest grammatical unit in a language. In other words, it is the smallest meaningful unit of a language. The linguistics field of study dedicated to morphemes is called morphology. A morpheme is not identical to a word, and the principal difference between the two is that a morpheme may or may not stand alone, whereas a word, by definition, is freestanding. When a morpheme stands by itself, it is considered as a root because it has a meaning of its own (e.g. the morpheme cat) and when it depends on another morpheme to express an idea, it is an affix because it has a grammatical function (e.g. the -- s in cats to indicate that it is plural). Every word comprises one or more morphemes.","Words – Doowans News&Events Skip to content Sticks and Stones can break my Bones, But Words can never hurt me Words are powerful I remember say this when I was younger. I woke up this morning with this saying going through my head. Welcome to the world of words. Words have a life of their own. Just like Sticks and Stones, Words can hurt. Everything reflected in reality, comes from words. If we don’t have a word for it, we make one up. Ideas, thoughts, actions, emotions, feelings, everything one can imagine and express has a word attached to it. In language, a word is the smallest element that may be uttered in isolation with semantic or pragmatic content (with literal or practical meaning) Meaning expressed without Words In the Beginning of Words In the beginning there was the word and the word was… So the word was the beginning. Words must be important. Yet we use them as if they don’t have any power. When if fact, words are powerful vibrations the create powerful effects. The creator used word vibrations in the creation. Words must be tools for creation for without them nothing would exist. In fact, “the” is the most used word in The Bible. In the English language, words come from the combining of 26 characters. These characters are like characters in a stage play. All taking part in creating a scene by playing their parts. Some characters are given more importance with capitalization and marks that are called accents. These characters play their roles in a play called life. As we sit back and watch the play unfold it’s meaning. A word may consist of a single morpheme. In linguistics, a morpheme is the smallest grammatical unit in a language. The field of study dedicated to morphemes is called morphology. A morpheme is not identical to a word, and the principal difference between the two is that a morpheme may or may not stand alone, where a word, by definition, is freestanding. Every word comprises one or more morphemes. What important to note here is the word morpheme. The root word is morph, to be transformed. Breaking the word down to its two components it’s Morph -e- me. One can extract the meaning of this word as Transform me. Words have the power to transform using morphology. Words can be put together to build larger elements of language, such as phrases. The term word may refer to a spoken word or to a written word, or sometimes to the abstract concept behind either. Spoken words are made up of units of sound vibration called phonemes, and written words of symbols called graphemes, such as the letters of the English alphabet, a minimal unit of a writing system. A unit of a writing system consisting of all the written symbols or sequences of written symbols that are used to represent a single phoneme. Phoneme, to make a sound, are any of a small set of sound units, usually about 20 to 60 in number, and different for each language, considered to be the basic distinctive units of speech sound by which morphemes, words, and sentences are r [SEP_DOC] Language - Things and Stuff Wiki Jump to content From Things and Stuff Wiki this is an organically evolving personal wiki-form knowledge base, with on-the-fly/twenty years of copy-edited n otherwise curated patchworks of folksnomies n headings, containing trails n spirals of topics, descriptions, notes, breadcrumbs n stubs, links to sites, systems, software, manuals, organisations, people, articles, guides, slides, papers, books, comments, videos, screencasts, webcasts, scratchpads, etc | content is orientated towards mostly free/libre/open, mostly Linux | quality and age varies drastically | sometimes old things are first, sometimes last | Ctrl + mouse wheel to zoom in if text is too small | use the Table of Contents menu to navigate long pages | use the header -ToC links to shrink n expand the menu | link rot? Wayback Machine! | probably need to fix the theme CSS after an update | Chat to msg me (this I am not checking atm) | e Resources Weather Edinburgh Scotland Smiley / Lorem About / ToDo Meta / misc Maths Statistics Science Physics Biology Geology Space Time Body Breath Sleep Action Yoga Food Drink Drugs Lifelog Being Learning Language Buddhism Meditation Grounding Animals Genealogy Living Horticulture Waste Household Camping Travel Cycling Mapping Organising Media Digital lit. Design Politics / p Free/open Volly Guide Games Shopping Making Crafting Fibre / Fabric Woodwork Metalwork 3D printing Cloak design Fire brand Literature Technology Mechanics Equipment Electrical Radio / TV Signal Type / Emoji Computing Computer / CA Keyboard Compile / build Debugging Testing OSs / *nix / CLI Distros / Packages Android / Apps Apple / Windows Amiga / Emulation Console Automation Data / Open Documents Markdown Semantic Database Compre